~ magenta_c_miss
set mm_magenta_round += 1
damage 1d6 psychic party
Narrator: The words miss their mark, and the hungry dead bend the flame into a leering skull juggling loaded bones that crumble into ash. The bitter shriek that follows drives a cold nail behind your eyes, and you all flinch back.
-> magenta_rite


~ magenta_dies
Narrator: The last grain of your minute slips away, and the magenta fire gutters down into cold, greasy soot. Yorgrim reaches a grey hand into his own ribs and drops a pale shard of himself onto the dying ash.
set mm_soul_fragments += 1
set mm_rite_fails += 1
disadvantage checks 1h: a piece of your soul is in the fire
set mm_magenta_round = 0
set mm_magenta_succ = 0
set mm_magenta_bright = 0
if flag.mm_rite_fails >= 2
set mm_yorgrim_spent
yorgrim [weary]: I will feed more of my own marrow to the iron so the fire asks less of your voices next time. My edges grow thin in this mist, and the stone on my back feels heavier by the hour.
else
yorgrim [neutral]: The ember still holds a spark under the soot, so draw your breath and try the words again without blame.
endif
-> END

~ magenta_done
set beacon_magenta = 1
if flag.mm_magenta_bright >= 3
set beacon_magenta = 2
endif
if flag.mm_magenta_bright >= 6
set beacon_magenta = 3
endif
set mm_beacons_lit += 1
set mm_magenta_done
cutscene mm_beacon_lit
Narrator: A pillar of magenta flame roars toward the leering moon, burning the shroud back from the wet flagstones of the Mausoleum Lanes. Far across the cemetery, a solitary sconce catches with bright purple fire above the great sepulcher doors.
yorgrim [smile]: Briggsy always swore he could cheat the devil with a wink and a turned card. You gave him back his good name, and the old pirate would have tipped his hat to you.
if flag.mm_beacons_lit >= 5
set sepulcher_open
if not quest.maidenmist_dead_rise >= sepulcher
quest maidenmist_dead_rise sepulcher
endif
yorgrim [weary]: All five beacons burn beside my azure flame, and the iron locks on the sepulcher doors have finally given way. Come back to the centre with me now, for it is time to finish what remains.
endif
-> END


~ emerald_start
if flag.mm_emerald_guardians_dead
-> emerald_honor
endif
Narrator: You stand before the frost-rimmed iron brazier among the mossy humps of the Burial Mounds, breathing the rot of wet leaves and turned earth. Only the pale beam of the lantern in Isolde's jaws keeps the pressing shroud from your throats.
yorgrim [weary]: Farryn wore the antlered crown of the old circle and kept the Green Queen's laws in the deep timber. No wolf nor wren feared her step, and even the tricksy Fey spoke softly when she passed. Yet she loved Gwenna, a mortal woman of the Patient Lady, more than all the wild boughs of the wood.
* [if item:mm_oak_branch] Place the kindling in the brazier -> emerald_kindle
* I have no kindling yet -> emerald_nokindle
* Tell us again what you know of Farryn -> emerald_start
* We will be back -> END

~ emerald_nokindle
yorgrim [neutral]: The dry boughs for this bowl are tucked somewhere among these mounds, though my hollow head forgets which stone hides them. Look again through the brambles, for nothing is lost while the lantern burns.
-> END

~ emerald_kindle
take mm_oak_branch
set mm_emerald_kindled
Narrator: The dry twigs catch with a scent of crushed pine, and a sharp emerald flame climbs from the iron bowl. At once the cold fog thickens like curdled milk, and root-bound corpses drag themselves from the turf to be fed to the fire.
yorgrim [angry]: Stay within the ring of the hound's lantern and cut these husks down where they stand. Their dry bones must break inside the emerald light, or the brazier will go cold.
combat mm_emerald_rite

~ emerald_honor
if not flag.mm_emerald_began
set mm_emerald_began
set mm_emerald_round = 0
set mm_emerald_succ = 0
set mm_emerald_bright = 0
endif
-> emerald_rite

~ emerald_rite
if flag.mm_emerald_succ >= 6
-> emerald_done
endif
if flag.mm_yorgrim_spent and flag.mm_emerald_succ >= 4
-> emerald_done
endif
if flag.mm_emerald_round >= 10
-> emerald_dies
endif
Narrator: The green fire sways in the damp draft, waiting to hear whether you will honour Farryn's oath to the Old Ways, her gentle hand with beast and bough, or the mortal heart she loved above the forest.
